!deck: Math Identities (Cloze)
!tags: Math Trigonometric Hyperbolic Identity
!type: Cloze Math

!id: zvAyOSCTQW
!text:
\[
{{c2::e^{ix}::\ldots}}
=
{{c1::\cos x + i \sin x::\ldots}}
\]

Euler's Formula
~~~

!id: Rjr9k6j6ba
!text:
\[
{{c2::e^{-ix}::\ldots}}
=
{{c1::\cos x - i \sin x::\ldots}}
\]

Euler's Formula
~~~

!id: inJ6BD8ofs
!text:
\[
{{c2::\left( \cos(x) + i \sin(x) \right)^n::\ldots}}
=
{{c1::\cos(nx) + i \sin(nx)::\ldots}}
\]

De Moivre's Formula
~~~

!id: kBXzrvvubz
!text:
\[
{{c2::z^n = 1::\ldots}}
\iff
{{c1::z = e^{2\pi i k / n}, \quad k = 0, 1, \ldots, n-1::\ldots}}
\]

\( n \)th Roots of Unity
!back extra: Write \( z = re^{i\theta} \), so \( z^n = r^n e^{in\theta} \). Matching this to \( 1 = e^{i \cdot 2\pi k} \) forces \( r = 1 \) and \( n\theta = 2\pi k \) for some integer \( k \), i.e. \( \theta = 2\pi k / n \). Taking \( k = 0, 1, \ldots, n-1 \) gives all \( n \) distinct roots; larger \( k \) only repeats these by periodicity of \( e^{i\theta} \).
~~~
